assertion  XBD.sys_socket.h.011
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION/SHUT_RD
class      required
since      2001
says       SHUT_RD, SHUT_RDWR and SHUT_WR are defined with three different
           values.
test       t/011-shut-values.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.sys_socket.h.012
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION/CMSG_FIRSTHDR
class      required
since      2001
says       CMSG_FIRSTHDR(mhdr) returns a null pointer if msg_controllen is zero
           or less than sizeof(struct cmsghdr), and otherwise a pointer of type
           struct cmsghdr * to the start of the control data (msg_control).
test       t/012-cmsg-firsthdr.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.sys_socket.h.013
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION/CMSG_NXTHDR
class      required
since      2001
says       For control data holding two objects laid out CMSG_SPACE apart,
           CMSG_NXTHDR(mhdr, first) returns the second header and
           CMSG_NXTHDR(mhdr, second) a null pointer; CMSG_NXTHDR(mhdr, first) is
           a null pointer when the first object is the only one.
uses       XBD.sys_socket.h.012
note       The second header is placed CMSG_SPACE(sizeof(int)) after the first,
           the space an object of that data length takes, and both headers are
           filled in before CMSG_NXTHDR is called, so the unspecified case of an
           incomplete next header does not arise.
test       t/013-cmsg-nxthdr.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.sys_socket.h.016
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION/CMSG_NXTHDR
class      required
since      2024
says       CMSG_NXTHDR(mhdr, cmsg) with a null pointer of type struct cmsghdr *
           for cmsg is equivalent to CMSG_FIRSTHDR(mhdr), for control data of
           room for a header and for none.
uses       XBD.sys_socket.h.012
note       Kept apart from XBD.sys_socket.h.013: an implementation that follows
           the null pointer would crash this test alone.
test       t/016-cmsg-nxthdr-null.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.sys_socket.h.014
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION/CMSG_LEN
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION/CMSG_SPACE
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION/CMSG_DATA
class      required
since      2024
says       For a constant length CMSG_LEN and CMSG_SPACE are integer constant
           expressions; for each length n, CMSG_LEN(n) is at least sizeof(struct
           cmsghdr) + n, CMSG_SPACE(n) is at least CMSG_LEN(n) and a multiple of
           the alignment of struct cmsghdr; CMSG_DATA(cmsg) has type unsigned
           char * and points after the header at an offset where n data bytes fit
           within CMSG_LEN(n).
uses       XBD.sys_socket.h.012
note       CMSG_SPACE keeps the next header aligned, which is what padding to
           satisfy alignment requirements is for. The data offset is not fixed by
           the page, so only that the data array lies after the header and inside
           cmsg_len is checked.
test       t/014-cmsg-len-space.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.sys_socket.h.015
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION/SCM_RIGHTS
class      required
since      2001
says       SCM_RIGHTS can be stored in the int cmsg_type member and SOL_SOCKET in
           the int cmsg_level member of a struct cmsghdr and read back unchanged.
note       That the pair means that the data holds file descriptors is tested on
           sendmsg() and recvmsg().
test       t/015-scm-rights.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

